Z||000000||Gasolene||02-17-2001||09:30 PM||||I need to know how to change a fuel filter & pump.  I not very little about Fiero mechnics.  My engine died as I was driving home yesterday, It cranks fine but wont start.(I assume its fuel filter or pump).<BR>Is there any easy diagnostic tests I can do.||||reg||1
Z||000001||FieroJoe||02-17-2001||10:44 PM||||L4 or V6??  If it is a V6, there is a schrader valve on the fuel rail to pressure check, I believe (I have a L4).<P>Otherwise, the first thing you can do is get a Haynes Automotive Repair Manual for your fiero, Autozone has them (about $15).  It shows the location and steps to changing your filter, and also how to drop the tank and remove the fuel pump/sender unit.  Knowledge is one of the most indespensible tools around, you'll learn alot from that book!<P><P>------------------<BR>FieroJoe<BR>c311t@yahoo.com<BR>AIM: Fiero87311, Snowboarder0311<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/profile_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||Gasolene||02-17-2001||11:44 PM||||sorry, I guess it would help if you knew what kind of Fiero I have.<P>1984 2M4<BR>4cyl 2.5||||reg||1
Z||000003||AL||02-18-2001||12:10 AM||||first off,do you hear the fuel pump coming on when you first try to start car?second did you check your fuses?<BR>if both look and hear correctly,then go to your engine and you will see a little silver cannister next to the throtle body,unloosen one of the lines to see if your getting fuel,if fuel spills then you know that you are getting gas and your problem would be spark not fuel,also does the car have gas,the reason i say this,is because my indy didn't want to start and my guage was telling m it was full,but when i dropped tank there was no gas to be seen.good luck,<B>AL</B> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/AL_sig.gif"> <BR>86gtfastback<BR>84 indy fiero<P>------------------<BR> ||||reg||1
Z||000004||fierosound||02-18-2001||01:01 AM|||| <A HREF="http://members.nbci.com/fierov6/OSG/osg.html" TARGET=_blank>http://members.nbci.com/fierov6/OSG/osg.html</A> <P>This is a pretty good online service guide for those who haven't yet purchased a Fiero repair manual...||||reg||1
Z||000005||Gasolene||02-18-2001||02:54 PM||||I checked the ECM fuse, and Fuel Pump fuse, they are fine.  Also, when I took off the air cleaner, there was gas spray so I think the problem is with the spark.<BR>Is there any easy diagnostics tests I can do???<P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.geocities.com/fiero_stereo/thumbsig.jpg">    <BR><A HREF="http://www.angelfire.com/ab4/fierostereo/index.html" TARGET=_blank>My Web Page</A><BR>Edmonton Fiero Club||||reg||1
Z||000006||FieroJoe||02-18-2001||05:44 PM||||well if now the problem is spark, check and make sure that all the spark plug wires are connected good to the distributor cap and the plugs themselves.  Since the whole car died, and it wasnt just a misfire, I would think that the wire from the coil to the distributor cap is either bad or had come loose.  Check all those electrical connections.  All the other potential problems with the ignition that I could think of, would have tell tale signs of impending failure, where as usually when it is all of a sudden that something doesnt work, a loose connection could have been the only problem.||||reg||1
Z||000007||286SE'S||02-21-2001||03:12 PM||||Sounds like a failed ignition module to me. I have had the same part fail twice and now carry a spare and the tools to change it in my trunk. A tell-tale sign that the module has failed is no movement on the tach when cranking. Usually there is a little bit of a jump when you engage the starter. With a dead module..nothing.<BR>Terry<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000008||DavidHenshaw||02-21-2001||07:59 PM||||The most likely suspect is the ignition module. Its just below the distributor, remove it and take it to an auto parts store NAPA, Advance etc and they can test it for you.<P>If its not the module then it may be ignition coil, or it could be the distributor itself. If there is rust present in the distributor its time to replace. <P>Of course the first thing you could do is remove the distributor cap and observe the rotor while a friend is making the starter turn the engine over. If the rotor doesn't turn as your friend makes the engine crank then you have either a broken camshaft or a broken timing chain. These last two are unusual. Bottom line, you probably have a bad ignition module, coil or distributor.<P>Dave<P>86 GT||||reg||1
Z||000009||pontiacs4me||02-23-2001||02:16 AM||||Also if the rotor doesnt turn the roll pin in the distributor gear could of sheared off.  I had that happen to me 2 years ago on the hot rod power tour, just outside of topeka kansas.  It only cost 9 cents to fix but it wasted about 1.5 hours of my life.||||reg||1
Z||000010||Carrolles||02-23-2001||07:18 AM||||Rust in the distributor isn't necessarily a bad thing (although not a good thing either).  I'd bet that 90% of the Fieros on the road have rusty reluctors (the pointy vertical metal tabs) in the distributors.  The module picks up the disturbance in the magnetic field when when the reluctor passes by.  Surface rust has little effect on this function.||||reg||1
Z||000011||Gasolene||02-23-2001||02:11 PM||||It was the ingition module.  I took it to the shop.  The total came to $192.00 canadian.  Is this a good price or not?  the module was $82.||||reg||1
